{
  "topic": "Types of Fractions",
  "affirmation": "You turned shapes and a cake example into accessible fraction thinking.",
  "identity": "You teach with simple visuals and everyday language: you showed circles, rectangles, and a cake, then asked students to count and name parts. You pair clear math words (numerator, denominator) with warm, real-world hooks so the idea feels both concrete and friendly.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Pivot to partitioning",
      "quote": "کتنے پارٹس بنیں گے آپ کے حساب سے؟",
      "why": "This moved the class from naming a shape to thinking about parts of a whole, and a student immediately answered by counting the parts."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cake anchor",
      "quote": "پارٹ اس لیے لاتے ہیں اور ایکول پارٹ لاتے ہیں تاکہ اس میں لائن نہ آئے۔",
      "why": "You used the fairness-of-sharing cake image to make the math meaningful and to connect partitioning to how students already think about dividing things."
    },
    {
      "title": "Clear math language",
      "quote": "جو اوپر جو نمبر ہوتا ہے، اس کو کیا کہتے ہیں؟ نمبریٹر۔",
      "why": "You introduced exact vocabulary and invited students to say it aloud, which strengthened their language for talking about fractions."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Student engagement",
  "strength_note": "You kept students active and speaking — for example you had them count parts aloud and chorally identify numerator and denominator.",
  "horizon_title": "Two task levels",
  "horizon_note": "Design two levels of the same activity (one slightly easier, one slightly harder) so students who finish early or need more support can work at the right level during the same lesson.",
  "journey_note": "In this lesson you guided students from shape recognition to 'proper fraction' language and hit a solid 71% peak by repeating clear moves and checks.",
  "score_framing": "A 71% in one observed lesson shows strong routines and real momentum — it's a helpful milestone on a longer climb, not a final judgement.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you used open prompts and real examples (for example asking \"ٹھیک ہے۔ لیکن اس کی ڈیفینیشن کوئی بتا سکتا ہے؟ ہوتا کیا ہے؟\") — it's not full marks because students were not offered choices of methods nor asked to select or justify a way to solve.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because students used different phrasings and summarized the idea aloud (for example \"جو کلر کیا ہوا ہے، وہ اوپر ہے، اور ٹوٹل نیچے ہے\") — it's not full marks because students only asked procedural questions such as \"میم، میں کروں؟\" and did not ask content-focused why/how questions.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is strong because you used accurate vocabulary and corrected student placements of numerator and denominator (for example \"9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟\") — it's not full marks because you did not present a non-routine problem or allow sustained think time for students to wrestle with misconceptions."
  }
}
